// Methods (owner, Nov 2026): visual checks of pipework, pressure testing, lifting floorboards,
// small openings in the ceiling below laminate/tiled floors, insurance leak reports.
// No thermal, acoustic or tracer gas kit is claimed. Do not add any.
// Prices: first hour £120 weekdays 9am-4pm, £150 evenings/nights/weekends, £180 bank holidays, no call-out fee.

const packages = [
  { id: 'weekday', tier: 'Weekdays 9am to 4pm', name: 'Leak finding and repair', price: '£120', unit: 'first hour, inc VAT',
    points: ['No call-out fee', 'Finding the leak comes first', 'Extra time priced before we carry on'] },
  { id: 'out-of-hours', tier: 'Evenings, nights and weekends', popular: true, name: 'Emergency leak call-out', price: '£150', unit: 'first hour, inc VAT',
    points: ['No call-out fee', 'We answer 24/7, Sundays too', 'Water isolated first to stop the damage'] },
  { id: 'bank-holiday', tier: 'Bank holidays', name: 'Bank holiday leak call-out', price: '£180', unit: 'first hour, inc VAT',
    points: ['No call-out fee', 'Same team, same careful work', 'Parts priced before they are fitted'] },
];

const faqs = [
  { q: 'How much does it cost to find a leak in Liverpool?', a: 'There is no call-out fee. The first hour is £120 on weekdays 9am to 4pm, £150 on evenings, nights and weekends, and £180 on bank holidays. All prices include VAT. Any extra time or parts are priced before we carry on.' },
  { q: 'How do you find a hidden leak?', a: 'We check the pipework, joints, valves and appliances you can see first, as most leaks start there. We can pressure test the pipework to confirm whether a section is leaking. Then we lift floorboards along the pipe run and follow the water back to the source. Under laminate or a tiled bathroom, a small opening in the ceiling below is often cheaper. We agree with you what needs opening up before we start.' },
  { q: 'Do you provide leak reports for insurance claims?', a: 'Yes. We can write a leak report for your insurer. It covers what caused the leak, where it was and what we repaired, so you have the evidence for your claim.' },
  { q: 'Water is coming through my ceiling. What do I do?', a: 'Turn the water off at the stopcock, usually under the kitchen sink. If water is near a light fitting, do not use that light. Switch it off at the fuse box if you can do so safely. Put a bucket under the drip, then ring us on 0151 558 0334. Most ceiling leaks come from a bath, shower, toilet or pipe joint in the room above.' },
  { q: 'How can I tell if I have a hidden leak?', a: 'Turn off every tap and appliance that uses water, then look at your water meter. If the numbers still move, water is escaping somewhere. Other signs are a damp patch that keeps growing or a musty smell. Boiler pressure that keeps dropping, or a water bill that has gone up for no reason, are signs too.' },
  { q: 'Can you find a leak under a concrete floor?', a: 'Leaks under solid concrete floors or deep inside walls are harder to reach than leaks under floorboards. We look at the pipe runs and tell you honestly what we can find and what it would take. If a leak needs specialist detection equipment, we tell you before you spend money.' },
  { q: 'Will you have to damage my floor or ceiling?', a: 'Sometimes a board has to come up or a small hole has to be cut. We keep it as small as we can, in the right place, and agree it with you first. Floorboards go back down after the repair.' },
  { q: 'Is the leak my responsibility or United Utilities?', a: 'Usually, the pipe from your property boundary into the house is yours. The pipe from the water main up to the boundary is usually United Utilities. Shared supplies can be different. If the leak is outside your boundary, call United Utilities.' },
  { q: 'Can you fix the leak on the same visit?', a: 'Most of the time, yes. Most leaks come from a joint, valve, waste pipe, flexi pipe or appliance. If a part or a bigger job is needed, you get the price first.' },
  { q: 'I am not at the property. Can you still help?', a: 'Yes. Landlords and owners often are not there when a leak starts. We can arrange access, and we can send you photos of what caused the leak and the repair, as several of our Google reviews mention.' },
  { q: 'Do you come out at night and at weekends?', a: 'Yes. We answer 24 hours a day, 7 days a week, including bank holidays. We are typically with you within 30 minutes. Reviews mention us arriving within 20 minutes for leaks.' },
  { q: 'My boiler pressure keeps dropping. Is that a leak?', a: 'Often, yes. Water is escaping from the heating system, perhaps at a radiator valve, a joint or a pipe under the floor. We find it the same way. See our boiler repair page if the fault turns out to be inside the boiler.' },
  { q: 'Can water from a leak affect my electrics?', a: 'Yes. Keep away from wet lights and sockets. If water has reached them, our partner firm Ideal Electrical Solutions, a 24/7 emergency electrician, can make the electrics safe.' },
];
---

