  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="leaking-now">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What should I do if water is leaking now?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Turn the water off at the stopcock, keep away from wet electrics, then ring us on <a href="tel:01515580334">{PHONE}</a>. These steps limit the damage while we are on the way.</p>
      <div class="brx-danger"><p><strong>Water near lights or sockets?</strong> Do not touch them. Do not use a wet light. Switch the power off at the fuse box only if you can reach it safely and stay dry.</p></div>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Turn off the stopcock.</strong> It is usually under the kitchen sink. Turn it clockwise until it stops.</li>
        <li><strong>Open the cold taps.</strong> This drains the water left in the pipes, so less comes through.</li>
        <li><strong>Catch the drips.</strong> Put a bucket or bowl under the leak. Move rugs, electrics and anything precious.</li>
        <li><strong>Ceiling bulging?</strong> Keep out from under it. A sagging ceiling can come down.</li>
        <li><strong>Call us.</strong> We answer 24/7 and are typically with you within 30 minutes.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Cannot find the stopcock, or it will not turn? Tell us when you call. We can talk you through it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="cost">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What do I pay for leak detection in Liverpool?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">You pay for the first hour, with no call-out fee. That is £120 on weekdays 9am to 4pm, £150 on evenings, nights and weekends, and £180 on bank holidays, all including VAT.</p>
      <p>If we need longer than the first hour, we tell you and price it before we carry on. The same goes for parts.</p>
      <h3>What is included</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Stopping the water first, so the damage stops spreading</li>
        <li>Checking the pipes, joints, valves and appliances you can see</li>
        <li>A pressure test where it helps to narrow things down</li>
        <li>Opening up only what we agree with you</li>
        <li>Fixing the leak and testing the repair</li>
        <li>A 12-month workmanship guarantee on our repair</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="methods">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How do you find a hidden leak?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The practical way. We check what can be seen, pressure test the pipes, then open up the right spot and follow the water back to its source.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Visual checks</h3>
          <p>We check joints, valves, waste pipes, flexi pipes, toilets, baths, showers and radiators. Most leaks start here, so this often finds it fast.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Pressure testing</h3>
          <p>We test the pipework under pressure. If the pressure falls, there is a leak. It also helps us narrow down which section it is in.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Lifting floorboards</h3>
          <p>We lift boards along the pipe run and trace the water back to the source. The boards go back down after the repair.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>A small ceiling opening</h3>
          <p>Under laminate or a tiled bathroom floor, a small hole in the ceiling below is often cheaper than ripping up the floor.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">No guesswork and no smashing walls on a hunch. We follow the water.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Leak under concrete or deep in a wall?</strong> These are harder to reach than leaks under boards. We tell you honestly what we can find. If a leak needs specialist detection equipment, we say so before you spend money.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="damage">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Will you need to damage my floors or walls?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Sometimes a little, but never without asking. We agree what needs opening up before we start, and we keep it as small as we can.</p>
      <p>Floorboards lift out and go back down. Laminate and tiles are harder. They can crack or will not go back the same. That is why we often go in from the ceiling below. A small, neat hole in a ceiling is cheaper to make good than a new bathroom floor.</p>
      <p>We always pick the spot with you. You know what each choice will mean before anything is cut.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a leak detection visit work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Most visits take one to two hours from arrival to a fixed leak. Here is what happens.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>You call us.</strong> Tell us what you can see. We give you the first-hour price there and then. Typically we are with you within 30 minutes.</li>
        <li><strong>Stop the water.</strong> If it is still leaking, we isolate the supply first.</li>
        <li><strong>Check what can be seen.</strong> Joints, valves, waste pipes, flexi pipes, toilets, baths, showers and radiators.</li>
        <li><strong>Pressure test.</strong> Where it helps, we test the pipework to confirm the leak and narrow down the section.</li>
        <li><strong>Open up the right spot.</strong> We lift floorboards or cut a small ceiling opening, as agreed with you, and trace the water to its source.</li>
        <li><strong>Fix it and show you.</strong> We repair the leak and test it. If you are not there, we send photos of the cause and the repair.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Leaking right now?</strong> Turn off the stopcock and ring us. We answer 24/7.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="signs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How do I know if I have a hidden leak?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Do the meter test. Turn off every tap and appliance that uses water, then look at your water meter. If the numbers still move, water is escaping somewhere.</p>
      <h3>Other signs of a leak</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li><strong>Water through the ceiling.</strong> Usually a bath, shower, toilet or pipe joint in the room above.</li>
        <li><strong>A damp patch that keeps growing.</strong> On a wall, floor or ceiling. Often a slow leak from a joint or a corroded pipe.</li>
        <li><strong>Boiler pressure keeps dropping.</strong> Water is escaping from the heating system.</li>
        <li><strong>A wet floor by the sink, toilet or bath.</strong> Often a split waste pipe, a worn seal or a corroded flexi pipe behind a panel.</li>
        <li><strong>The sound of running water.</strong> When nothing is on. Often a pipe under the floor or a toilet cistern.</li>
        <li><strong>A musty smell.</strong> Damp that you cannot see yet.</li>
        <li><strong>A water bill that has jumped.</strong> With no change in how you use water.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The sooner a leak is found, the less damage it does. Even a slow drip can rot boards and stain ceilings over time.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="source">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Where is my leak coming from?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Where you see the water gives a good clue. This table shows the usual causes we find in Liverpool homes.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table sg-cmp stk">
          <thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td data-label="What you see"><strong>Drip or stain on a ceiling</strong></td><td data-label="Likely cause">Bath, shower, toilet or pipe joint in the room above</td><td data-label="What to do">Stopcock off, then call us</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="What you see"><strong>Boiler pressure dropping</strong></td><td data-label="Likely cause">Radiator valve, joint or heating pipe under the floor</td><td data-label="What to do">Call us. See <a href="/boiler-repair-liverpool">boiler repair</a> if it is the boiler</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="What you see"><strong>Water under the sink</strong></td><td data-label="Likely cause">Flexi pipe, tap connection or waste pipe</td><td data-label="What to do">Turn off the small valve on the pipe, then call us</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="What you see"><strong>Puddle by a radiator</strong></td><td data-label="Likely cause">Valve, joint or a corroded radiator</td><td data-label="What to do">See <a href="/radiators-liverpool">radiator repairs</a></td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="What you see"><strong>Meter moving, nothing wet</strong></td><td data-label="Likely cause">Hidden pipe, toilet cistern or the supply pipe outside</td><td data-label="What to do">Call us to trace it</td></tr>
            <tr><td data-label="What you see"><strong>Burst pipe or flood</strong></td><td data-label="Likely cause">Split or frozen pipe</td><td data-label="What to do">Stopcock off. See our <a href="/">24/7 emergency plumber</a> page</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Cold snap? Read our <a href="/blog/frozen-pipes-liverpool-winter-guide">frozen pipes guide</a> to stop pipes splitting in the first place.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Water reached your lights or sockets?</strong> Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/emergency-electrician">Ideal Electrical Solutions, a 24/7 emergency electrician</a>, can make the electrics safe.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="whose">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Is the leak on my side or United Utilities' side?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Usually, the pipe from your property boundary into the house is yours. The pipe from the water main up to your boundary is usually United Utilities'.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table defl">
          <thead><tr><th>Where the leak is</th><th>Usually whose job?</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Inside your home</strong></td><td>Yours. We can find and fix it.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Supply pipe from your boundary to the house</strong></td><td>Usually yours, as the owner.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Pipe in the street, up to your boundary</strong></td><td>Usually United Utilities. Call them.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Shared supplies, flats and terraces can be different. If you are not sure, call us first. We can tell you which side of the line the leak looks to be on.</p>
      <p>If you rent, tell your landlord or letting agent straight away. They are usually responsible for repairs to the pipes.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="insurance">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Can you write a leak report for my insurance claim?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Yes. We can write a leak report for your insurer. It covers what caused the leak, where it was and what we repaired.</p>
      <p>Many home insurance policies cover the cost of finding a hidden leak. This is often called "trace and access" cover. Check your policy, or ask your insurer, before you book if you can.</p>
      <h3>Tips for a smoother claim</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Take photos of the damage before anything is moved.</li>
        <li>Tell your insurer as soon as you can.</li>
        <li>Keep our invoice and the leak report together.</li>
        <li>Ask us for photos of the cause and the repair. We are happy to send them.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="jobs">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What leaks have you found for Liverpool customers?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">All sorts, from gushing sink pipes to slow leaks under the floor. These examples come from real Google reviews.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Gushing bathroom sink pipe</h3>
          <p>Tonia K was not at home. Part of the ceiling had already come down. We isolated the water, fixed the leak and cleaned up. The cause was a corroded flexi pipe. We sent photos of the cause, plus before and after shots.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Old pipe under the floor</h3>
          <p>James G had a leak in an old pipe under the flooring. We came the same day and found it. We gave options to fix it, came back the next day, and it was sorted.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Leaky pipe, dripping ceiling</h3>
          <p>Frank B had a leaky pipe and a dripping ceiling. We found the cause and fixed it in just over an hour.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Rental with water from the ceiling</h3>
          <p>Chris C owns a rental in Liverpool but lives elsewhere. He rang late in the evening about water coming through a ceiling. He says the costs were clear and very reasonable.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">Sara R had a kitchen leak. We were there within twenty minutes, dealt with it and gave advice on what to do next. <a href="https://www.google.com/search?q=Liverpool+Emergency+Plumber+reviews" target="_blank" rel="noopener">See all our reviews on Google</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

</ServiceGuide>
